sity Celebrates Convocation and Welcomes the Class of 2030 8 / 28 / 2026 2 : 06 PM Misericordia University Professor Selected for UC Berkeley Bridging Differences Fellowship 8 / 13 / 2026 10 : 03 AM Misericordia University Appoints Two New Members to Board of Trustees 7 / 30 / 2026 12 : 09 PM Admissions FAQ's More FAQ's What are the admission requirements? What type of financial aid is available? When should I apply? Who can I contact with questions about my application? What are the admission requirements? MU typically likes to see students with a history of proven success in the classroom with a minimum high school grade point average of 2.5. Minimum *SAT requirement is typically an 850 (Math + Critical Reading), but other factors can be taken into consideration. A number of programs have requirements for direct entry that are higher than university minimums. We advise checking the admission requirements for your program of interest within its designated section of the website. View our list of majors to find your program. *Note: Misericordia will be Test-Optional for Fall 2021 Admissions What type of financial aid is available? Misericordia offers merit-based scholarships for academics as well as community involvement, and need-based awards which can total well over $20,000 per year for qualified students. Visit our Financial Aid page to learn more . When should I apply? MU has a rolling admissions cycle, but we advise that students apply early in the cycle (Fall) as the space available in certain programs can be limited. Visit our Apply page to submit an application . Who can I contact with questions about my application? Your admissions counselor, or the Admissions Office at admiss@misericordia.edu or 1-866-262-6363. View our list of Admissions Staff to find your counselor. Misericordia University Dean Presents at tecBRIDGE Innovation Conference Post RSS Feeds Subscribe to Post Alerts Misericordia University Dean Presents at tecBRIDGE Innovation Conference 9 / 4 / 2026 9 : 26 AM Todd Shawver Discusses Higher Education’s Role in Building Pennsylvania’s Future Energy Workforce DALLAS, PA - Todd Shawver, dean of the College of Business at Misericordia University, recently presented at the tecBRIDGE Innovation Conference, contributing to conversations about the future of Pennsylvania’s energy industry and the workforce needed to support it. Shawver’s presentation, “Keeping the Lights On: Building Pennsylvania's Energy Workforce One Student at a Time,” explored how changes in technology, artificial intelligence, infrastructure investment and new energy demands are reshaping the industry and creating new workforce needs across Pennsylvania. Central to his presentation was the role colleges and universities can play in preparing students not only for existing jobs, but also for careers and opportunities that are still emerging. As industries continue to evolve, Shawver emphasized the importance of collaboration among higher education, employers, economic development organizations, entrepreneurs, government leaders and community partners. These relationships can help colleges and universities understand employers’ current needs while anticipating the skills and occupations that will be needed in the years ahead. The conference also reinforced the importance of stronger employer partnerships and more responsive approaches to workforce development. In addition to traditional degree programs, higher education institutions can respond to changing workforce needs through certificates, microcredentials, professional education and shorter-term training opportunities. “The conference reinforced the importance of bringing education, business, and community leaders together to create opportunities for our students and strengthen Northeastern Pennsylvania,” Shawver said. “I am grateful to Don Webster and the tecBridge team for the opportunity to participate.” Misericordia University President Daniel J. Myers, Ph.D.; James Roberts; Todd Sloan; Michelle Conway; and Selena Popek also attended the conference and supported Shawver’s presentation. Shawver noted that events such as the tecBRIDGE Innovation Conference provide an opportunity for higher education, business, government and community leaders to exchange ideas and build partnerships that can benefit students, employers and the region. The conversations align with the work of Misericordia University’s College of Business and its Center for Workforce and Talent Solutions, which seek opportunities to engage with industry and respond to evolving workforce needs. Shawver said he left the conference encouraged by the opportunities ahead for Northeastern Pennsylvania and the region’s ability to draw upon its educational institutions, businesses, entrepreneurs and communities. As Pennsylvania’s workforce needs continue to evolve, Shawver emphasized that higher education has an opportunity to work alongside industry and community partners to help prepare the workforce of the future while creating new opportunities for students and the communities they will serve. Share to Facebook Share to Twitter Share to LinkedIn Share to Pinterest Share to Email Print this page More News Stories Post RSS Feeds Subscribe to Post Alerts Play and Pause Buttons for Slideshow pause play Misericordia University Dean Presents at tecBRIDGE Innovation Conference Madonna Mantione Read More about Misericordia University Dean Presents at tecBRIDGE Innovation Conference Misericordia University Bridge Program Helps First-Year Students Prepare for College Success Madonna Mantione Read More about Misericordia University Bridge Program Helps First-Year Students Prepare for College Success Misericordia University Expands Nursing Pathways as Pennsylvania Addresses Workforce Shortage Madonna Mantione Read More about Misericordia University Expands Nursing Pathways as Pennsylvania Addresses Workforce Shortage Misericordia University Celebrates Convocation and Welcomes the Class of 2030 Mado